The code statements that lead to the bug are:
static int rt_mt6_check(const struct xt_mtchk_param *par) {
    const struct ip6t_rt *rtinfo = par->matchinfo;
    if (rtinfo->invflags & ~IP6T_RT_INV_MASK) { ... return -EINVAL; }
    /* No validation of rtinfo->addrnr */
    ...

for (i = 0; i < rtinfo->addrnr; i++) {
    /* e.g., compares rtinfo->addrs[i] with packet data */
}
Reason:
The `rt_mt6_check()` function, which is called during rule installation, did not verify that `addrnr` is within the allowed range (≤ `IP6T_RT_HOPS`). As a result, a rule with an arbitrary large `addrnr` could be accepted. Later, when `rt_mt6()` evaluates the rule, it uses `addrnr` as an upper bound to iterate over `rtinfo->addrs[]`, which has a fixed size of `IP6T_RT_HOPS`. An oversized `addrnr` causes out‑of‑bounds memory accesses, potentially leaking kernel data or causing a crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
+	if (rtinfo->addrnr > IP6T_RT_HOPS) {
+		pr_debug("too many addresses specified\n");
+		return -EINVAL;
+	}
Reason:
This added check in `rt_mt6_check()` rejects any rule where `addrnr` exceeds the maximum allowed number of addresses (`IP6T_RT_HOPS`). By performing the validation at rule installation time, malformed rules are prevented from being inserted. Consequently, when `rt_mt6()` later uses `addrnr` to index `rtinfo->addrs[]`, the index is guaranteed to be within the array bounds, eliminating the out‑of‑bounds vulnerability.

